{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And I will set my eye upon him</b> Rabbi Abraham said: the meaning is that I will look at him. But I have not found in Scripture the placing of the eye upon someone for seeing alone, as in \"And I will set My eye upon them for good\" (Jeremiah 24:6), \"Take him and set your eye upon him, and do nothing evil to him\" (ibid. 39:12) — for he did not command him to look at him after taking him, but rather to guard him and do him good. But Joseph vowed to them to show him compassion and to guard him, and if he did not mention it explicitly, it is because he abbreviated there in all these matters that Judah recounted before him, and he did not mention the imprisonment of Simeon or the accusation 'you are spies' — as a way of rebuke or through fear of the kingdom."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {0 "Rabbi Abraham refers to Ibn Ezra. The phrase דֶּרֶךְ מוּסָר could be read as Judah's rhetorical abbreviation (out of restraint/fear) or as Joseph's abbreviation in his words; the context and verb forms favor reading it as Judah's omission, though the syntax leaves the subject implicit."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:40.236001555Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}